  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: fcf7219bab7fe46a1ad266029b2fee19, type: 3}
  m_Name: Material Blending Documentation
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  icon: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 8b51422ff22a04c45a9716825c7ce074, type: 3}
  title: Material Blending Documentation
  sections:
  - heading: 
    text: "In this package, you will find several different Shadergraph shaders to
      be used for texture blending.  The shaders are made for easy expansion and
      quick edits. \n\nAlthough there are a couple of shaders which can be used right
      away, all of the example shaders are set up according to our pipeline.  So
      I encourage you to pick and choose between the multiple different methods of
      blending and construct a shader that is right for you.\nCurrently this pack
      does not have tesselation and displacement, because it is not yet supported
      by Shadergraph. However I will be updating this package when it finally arrives."
    linkText: 
    url: 
  - heading: Layered Blending
    text: "This shader blends multiple material layers based on height and a transition
      phase (an intensity-based mask).  The example shader has four different material
      layers which can be painted on the mesh using vertex colours. \nLayer 0
      is a base layer which is at all times unmasked.(Black in all channels)\nLayer
      1 uses the red channel.\nLayer 2 uses the green channel.\nLayer
      3 uses the blue channel.\n\nThe shader uses two subgraphs for easy expandability
      and ease of use.  The MaterialLayer subgraph handles the tiling of the material
      and sampling of the textures.  The MaterialBlending AOMixing does the blending
      using the subgraph HeightLerp and AOMixing.\n\nBlending materials work best
      when the heightmap is in a 0-1 range.  Other heightmaps still work but
      are likely to be more sensitive to value changes in the transition phase.\n\nCurrently,
      the shader is using the vertex colours for creating different masks for each
      layer.  However, the shader can easily be edited to be used in clown masked
      material workflows and splat map setups.  You only have to replace the transition
      phase input."
    linkText: 
    url: 
  - heading: Triplanar Layered Blending
    text: 'The example shader of this shader is very similar to the Layered Blending
      but it uses the subgraph MaterialLayer Triplanar.  This subgraph uses the standard
      shader graph triplanar node for sampling the material textures.


      One
      of the options lets you switch between the different spaces for the triplanar.
      Local space and world space are examples of this. However, this enum keyword
      variable is currently reacting a bit buggy and might not do anything. This
      might be fixed in a future update for shader graph.'
    linkText: 
    url: 
  - heading: AO Mixing
    text: 'Even though the regular height blending works great in a lot of cases,
      the two materials still feel somewhat disconnected from each other. This subgraph
      addresses a lot of these issues by providing extra AO near the transition height,
      simulating the contact area where contact shadows often occur.


      The
      main inputs for the AO Mixing are a heightmap and transition phase value. This
      will create a mask around the transition area and with two extra variables
      (range and strength) you can finetune the mixing.'
    linkText: 
    url: 
  - heading: Water Blending
    text: "A common trick to make your materials look more grounded and interesting
      is adding differences in roughness (smoothness).  The water blending example
      shader shows how to add water to a material layer setup.  The WaterLayer subgraph
      will blend a material layer with a body of water at a specific transition phase
      with our MaterialBlending and adds addition modifications on top. \n\nThe heightmap
      input is used to simulate the depth and colour of the water. We construct a
      mask for adding a wet edge at the transition phase by using the blended heightmap
      output. Because the WaterLayer subgraph acts the same as the MaterialBlending,
      you can just add it as another material layer."
    linkText: 
    url: 
  - heading: Tiling Break-up
    text: 'The last example shader shows a method to break-up texture tiling with
      non-regular textures. It works by sampling a texture twice and offsetting it
      by a low-frequency noise texture.

      Note that this will warp and distort
      the input texture a bit, hence this technique is not recommended for pattern-based
      textures such as bricks and tiles.


      The shader is based on a shader
      from Shadertoy and if you want to know how it works, I encourage you to check
      it out.'
    linkText: Fix mip texture tiling 2 taps
    url: https://www.shadertoy.com/view/WdVGWG
  - heading: Contact
    text: If you encountered any problems or want to know more about the inner workings
      of the shader, feel free to send me a mail regarding your specific issue.
    linkText: Contact mail
    url: mailto:Quint@emberglitch.com
  - heading: 
    text: Or join our Discord server where we can provide instant help and a learning
      environment.
    linkText: Discord
    url: https://discord.gg/wkGQh2r
  - heading: 
    text: All the textures used in this package are from the amazing Texture Haven
      website and repacked to fit our production pipeline.
    linkText: Texture Haven
    url: https://texturehaven.com/
  loadedLayout: 1
